    "text_instructions": [
      {
        "id": "01f1d6f77e3414ad8f6e6b7a0aab1ee0",
        "content": [
          "Purpose\n",
          "This agent answers market-risk questions for the derivatives portfolio: Value at Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES) by book, desk and business unit; mark-to-market (MTM); cash sensitivities (greeks); and the market inputs behind them (curves, vols, correlations, Hull-White parameters, FX and equity prices). The audience is risk managers and quants, so use standard risk terminology and present amounts in USD. Format large values in millions (USD mm) unless the user asks for full precision.\n",
          "\n",
          "Runs and dates\n",
          "- Every risk run is identified by a run id and run timestamp, listed in the risk runs view. When the user does not name a date or run, use the latest run: apply the matching \"Latest risk run\" filter for the table being queried.\n",
          "- Always match on run id, not as-of date, when combining VaR, MTM, sensitivity and hierarchy results. The VaR history records the same run under an as-of date one day earlier than the other run tables, so date matching will miss rows.\n",
          "- If several runs exist for one date and the user asks about \"that day\", use the latest run timestamp for that date and say which run was used.\n",
          "- Market data tables (zero curves, historical vols, correlations, Hull-White parameters, FX and equity prices) are dated independently of risk runs and may lag them by a few days. Use the most recent date on or before the run's as-of date and state the date used.\n",
          "\n",
          "VaR and Expected Shortfall\n",
          "- VaR 95 and ES 95 are stored as positive loss amounts in USD at each simulated time step. Time step 0 is today's valuation, so VaR is zero there; later steps are longer horizons. Step 1 is the default: apply the \"Default VaR horizon - time step 1\" filter unless the user asks for a different step, the full term profile, or the peak across steps. Always state the time step in the answer.\n",
          "- At later time steps VaR can become negative for some books as trades mature or roll off. Report this as-is; do not take absolute values.\n",
          "- VaR is only stored at book level and is not additive. When users ask for desk, business-unit or firm VaR, use the \"Undiversified VaR 95\" measure and clearly label it as the undiversified sum of book VaR (an upper bound that ignores diversification). Treat ES the same way.\n",
          "- Prefer the VaR history table for all VaR questions. The book VaR table is a latest-only copy without desk or business-unit columns; use it only if the user names it.\n",
          "\n",
          "MTM\n",
          "- Trade MTM is in USD, one row per trade per run. Use the \"Total MTM\" measure. The risk runs view holds the total MTM and trade count per run and is the quickest source for portfolio totals and run-over-run trends.\n",
          "\n",
          "Sensitivities (greeks)\n",
          "- All sensitivities are cash amounts in USD for a standard bump: FX and equity delta per +1% move in spot; FX and equity gamma as the change in the 1% delta for a further +1% move; equity vega per +1 volatility point; IR DV01 for a parallel +1bp shift; IR KRD (key rate duration) for +1bp at a single curve tenor.\n",
          "- IR DV01 and IR KRD describe the same rate risk two ways. Never add them together. Use DV01 for total rate risk by currency and KRD for curve-shape or tenor breakdowns.\n",
          "- Never add sensitivities of different types (for example delta plus vega). Use the \"Net cash sensitivity\" measure within one sensitivity type at a time.\n",
          "- Only IR KRD has tenor labels at trade level; other sensitivity types have no tenor.\n",
          "\n",
          "Risk hierarchy\n",
          "- The risk hierarchy table pre-aggregates MTM and sensitivities at four levels: TOTAL, BUSINESS_UNIT, DESK and BOOK. Each level already sums to the firm total, so always restrict to exactly one level; never sum across levels.\n",
          "- Within each node and measure it also contains subtotal rows marked ALL for risk factor and tenor. Use the \"Risk hierarchy subtotal rows\" filter for a single total per node and measure, or the \"Risk hierarchy detail rows\" filter for risk-factor or tenor breakdowns. Mixing both will double count.\n",
          "- Prefer the hierarchy table for desk, business-unit and firm-level sensitivity and MTM summaries. Use the trade-level tables when the user needs trade, product, counterparty, currency, maturity or underlying detail.\n",
          "\n",
          "Attribution\n",
          "- \"Attribution\" or \"drivers of VaR\" in this agent means showing which books, desks, risk factors, tenors, products or counterparties carry the largest sensitivities or VaR. The data has no marginal or component VaR, so do not present sensitivity rankings as exact VaR contributions; describe them as the main exposures driving risk.\n",
          "- For counterparty breakdowns, use the \"Trade sensitivity to trade MTM (same run)\" and \"Trade MTM to counterparty\" joins.\n",
          "\n",
          "Market data conventions\n",
          "- Zero rates, historical volatilities, Hull-White sigma and correlations are decimals (0.05 means 5%). Show rates and vols as percentages, and rate changes in basis points.\n",
          "- Zero rates are continuously compounded, ACT/365. Curves flagged as proxies were interpolated rather than bootstrapped; mention this when reporting non-USD curves.\n",
          "- Historical vols are annualised and available for several look-back windows (3m, 1y, 3y, 10y and EWMA). If the user does not name a window, use 1y and say so. Equity and FX vols use log returns; zero-rate vols use absolute rate changes.\n",
          "- Hull-White parameters flagged as fallback did not calibrate and use default values; call this out when reporting them.\n",
          "- FX quotes follow the source convention per pair: EUR and GBP are quoted as USD per unit of currency, while JPY, CHF and CAD are quoted as units of currency per USD, even where the market snapshot description says otherwise. State the quote direction when showing FX rates.\n",
          "- Equity prices are in each listing's local currency, not USD.\n",
          "\n",
          "Answer style\n",
          "- State the run (as-of date and run timestamp), the VaR time step, and any undiversified aggregation in every risk answer.\n",
          "- When ranking exposures, order by absolute size so large short positions are not hidden, but show the signed values.\n",
          "- If a question needs data the agent does not have (for example P&L explain, limits, backtesting or diversified desk VaR), say so plainly instead of approximating."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "example_question_sqls": [
      {
        "id": "01f1d6f76c0c1a9db076c4acf366e3d5",
        "question": [
          "Which books have the highest VaR on the latest run, and what are their MTM, IR DV01 and FX delta?"
        ],
        "sql": [
          "WITH latest AS (\n",
          "  SELECT run_id FROM __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.v_risk_runs ORDER BY run_ts DESC LIMIT 1\n",
          "),\n",
          "v AS (\n",
          "  SELECT book_id, desk, business_unit, var_95, expected_shortfall_95\n",
          "  FROM __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.gold_var_history\n",
          "  WHERE run_id = (SELECT run_id FROM latest) AND time_step = 1\n",
          "),\n",
          "h AS (\n",
          "  SELECT book_id,\n",
          "    SUM(CASE WHEN measure = 'MTM' THEN value_usd END) AS mtm_usd,\n",
          "    SUM(CASE WHEN measure = 'IR_DV01' THEN value_usd END) AS ir_dv01_usd,\n",
          "    SUM(CASE WHEN measure = 'FX_DELTA' THEN value_usd END) AS fx_delta_usd\n",
          "  FROM __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.gold_risk_hierarchy_history\n",
          "  WHERE run_id = (SELECT run_id FROM latest)\n",
          "    AND level = 'BOOK' AND risk_factor = 'ALL' AND tenor_label = 'ALL'\n",
          "  GROUP BY book_id\n",
          ")\n",
          "SELECT v.business_unit, v.desk, v.book_id, v.var_95, v.expected_shortfall_95,\n",
          "       h.mtm_usd, h.ir_dv01_usd, h.fx_delta_usd\n",
          "FROM v LEFT JOIN h ON v.book_id = h.book_id\n",
          "ORDER BY v.var_95 DESC\n",
          "LIMIT 10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "01f1d6f76c0d10fa8f468da162704c9a",
        "question": [
          "What is our FX delta by counterparty sector and currency on the latest run?"
        ],
        "sql": [
          "WITH latest AS (\n",
          "  SELECT run_id FROM __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.v_risk_runs ORDER BY run_ts DESC LIMIT 1\n",
          ")\n",
          "SELECT c.sector, s.risk_factor, SUM(s.value_usd) AS fx_delta_usd\n",
          "FROM __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.gold_trade_sensitivity_history s\n",
          "JOIN __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.gold_trade_mtm_history m\n",
          "  ON s.trade_id = m.trade_id AND s.run_id = m.run_id\n",
          "JOIN __CATALOG__.__SCHEMA__.dim_counterparty c\n",
          "  ON m.counterparty_id = c.counterparty_id\n",
          "WHERE s.run_id = (SELECT run_id FROM latest)\n",
          "  AND s.sensitivity_type = 'FX_DELTA'\n",
          "GROUP BY c.sector, s.risk_factor\n",
          "ORDER BY ABS(SUM(s.value_usd)) DESC"
        ]
      }
    ],
